---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ContrastChecker from '../../components/ContrastChecker.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="WCAG Color Contrast Checker" description="Check if a text/background color pair meets WCAG AA/AAA accessibility standards, with a live preview.">
  <ContrastChecker client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why contrast ratio matters</h2>
  <p>Low contrast between text and background makes content hard to read, especially for people with low vision. WCAG defines minimum contrast ratios for different text sizes and conformance levels (AA is the common baseline; AAA is stricter).</p>

  <h2>Common Contrast Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checking contrast only for body text</strong>, forgetting placeholder text, disabled states, and icons also need adequate contrast.</li>
    <li><strong>Using light gray text on white</strong> for a "subtle" look that fails contrast requirements for many users.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Contrast Checker</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Verifying a color combination meets accessibility standards before shipping a design</li>
    <li>Auditing an existing site's color choices for WCAG compliance</li>
    <li>Choosing between color options that both look good but differ in accessibility</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>WCAG Contrast FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the minimum contrast ratio for AA compliance?</strong> 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text (18pt+ or 14pt+ bold) under WCAG AA — AAA raises these to 7:1 and 4.5:1 respectively.</p>
  <p><strong>Does this check apply to placeholder text?</strong> WCAG technically exempts placeholder text and disabled UI from the same requirement, but many accessibility guidelines still recommend meeting AA contrast for placeholders since users do rely on reading them.</p>
</ToolLayout>
